   <indicator aggregation-status="1" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Number of people trained in FFS programmes in target communities (M/F)</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">FFS programs are defined as an experiential field-based training where farmers gather to discuss and learn about best-practices for crop management. Groups of farmers from the beneficiary registry are allocated to CIGs (common interest groups); each CIG will form the basis for each FFS. Each FFS (based on the specific common interests) will include 10-20 farmers on average. These are an extension approach that include: Training of Trainers / Training of Lead Farmers (facilitators). Afterward, Lead Farmers and extension staff train sub-farmers in communities. This indicator tracks a) the number of people directly trained by the project as TOT/TOF, and b) the number of additional farmers trained by the lead farmers. 

The trainings include: Pre-harvest (orchard establishment, orchard design/layout, summer/winter pruning and training, fertilizer application, irrigation methods, mulching compost preparation and usage, weed control, pest and disease management), Post-harvest (proper harvesting techniques, precooling, sorting, grading, packaging, handling, storage, transportation), Pesticide hazards, safe use of pesticides, and IPM training.</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline value="12005" year="2023"></baseline>
